# --- Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `.sops.` in a filename means encrypted. That is what makes this work: the encrypted and plaintext
# forms have DIFFERENT names, so a path rule can tell them apart. Deny everything under secrets/, then
# allow back only the two forms that are safe to commit.
#
#   secrets/demo-secrets.yaml         plaintext  -> ignored
#   secrets/demo-secrets.sops.yaml    encrypted  -> committed
#   secrets/demo-secrets.yaml.example placeholders -> committed
secrets/*
!secrets/*.sops.yaml
!secrets/*.yaml.example
!secrets/.gitkeep

# SOPS private keys never belong in the repo. The PUBLIC key in .sops.yaml is fine and is meant to be
# committed.
*.agekey
keys.txt
